WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:07.519
So replication, this is a concept that isn't particularly unique to Unreal.

2
00:00:07.519 --> 00:00:14.679
Obviously every online engine, every online game would have some kind of concept

3
00:00:14.679 --> 00:00:19.839
for data going from the server to the client,

4
00:00:19.839 --> 00:00:23.679
but replication in Unreal is kind of worth talking about

5
00:00:23.679 --> 00:00:29.300
because it's got a lot of unique components that are kind of like probably more Unreal only.

6
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.500
So what actually is replication?

7
00:00:31.539 --> 00:00:35.179
The term might be interchangeable, might be called other things,

8
00:00:35.179 --> 00:00:43.939
but replication is basically the process of data going from the server to the client.

9
00:00:43.939 --> 00:00:47.899
It gives server authority over client-side variables.

10
00:00:47.899 --> 00:00:56.060
And essentially what the server will do is frequently check for whether or not

11
00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:00.979
the information that the client has matches what's on the server.

12
00:01:02.469 --> 00:01:06.189
So networked actors have a kind of a net update frequency,

13
00:01:06.189 --> 00:01:09.829
and this is used to determine how often an actor replicates,

14
00:01:09.829 --> 00:01:12.150
so how often we make these checks,

15
00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.829
and the server will keep a kind of shadow set of data.

16
00:01:14.829 --> 00:01:21.989
So that's the server, as well as the server having the actual data for what the value actually is,

17
00:01:21.989 --> 00:01:27.390
it will keep a kind of copy for what the last thing it sent to each client was.

18
00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:31.750
And the server is going to compare that shadow data against its current set of values.

19
00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:33.829
And if there's a kind of mismatch, so if it's to say,

20
00:01:33.829 --> 00:01:38.230
oh, this data is clearly changed from what I last sent to the client,

21
00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:41.670
then it knows that it's going to need to kind of like send some information down to the client

22
00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:45.859
to bring them kind of back up to date with what the server's current idea of everything is.

23
00:01:45.859 --> 00:01:50.980
Obviously, this check comes with an inherent kind of network cost.

24
00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.579
So there are things we can definitely do to kind of reduce that cost

25
00:01:54.579 --> 00:01:57.819
and maybe not be making that check quite as often.

26
00:01:57.819 --> 00:02:01.980
The cheapest one to do is updating our net update frequency,

27
00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.900
which is essentially a little bit like reducing your tick time almost.

28
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:08.020
We've talked about doing that before as a way to kind of optimize your tick.

29
00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:11.580
You can basically just check for replication, less frequency,

30
00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:17.430
and that's through our net update frequency on a per-actor basis.

31
00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:19.750
I think we may get into this a little bit later,

32
00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.669
but it's something we've talked about before as well,

33
00:02:21.669 --> 00:02:26.629
is that actor is our kind of like main point for dealing with replication.

34
00:02:26.629 --> 00:02:31.069
Whilst components can be replicated to, actor is our kind of central entry point.

35
00:02:31.069 --> 00:02:35.830
So a lot of this stuff is stuff that a lot of these networked concepts that we talk about

36
00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:38.909
would be stuff that we do handle on the actor itself.

37
00:02:38.909 --> 00:02:41.590
Net update frequency is actually an example of that.

38
00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:44.150
In fact, you can just quickly jump into the engine.

39
00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.990
I'm in the level blueprint here, but level blueprint is actually an actor,

40
00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:49.110
so this should be here.

41
00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:51.590
Update frequency, yeah.

42
00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:53.550
Net update frequency, you can see it's there.

43
00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:57.889
This would be available in any actor.

44
00:02:57.889 --> 00:03:02.090
So, yeah, that's one of the quickest ways to kind of update this.

45
00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:10.009
The quickest ways to kind of like make your game more net performant

46
00:03:10.009 --> 00:03:14.169
is looking at your net update frequency.

47
00:03:14.169 --> 00:03:16.210
It's one of the lowest hanging fruits.

48
00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:21.169
It just comes back to that kind of, and a lot of this kind of network logic

49
00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:22.210
is really just about that.

50
00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:26.849
It's that kind of same core tenet of really all code optimization

51
00:03:26.849 --> 00:03:29.689
is that doing something less is better.

52
00:03:29.689 --> 00:03:33.520
The less you can do something, the better.

53
00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.039
One of the kind of important things to bear in mind

54
00:03:36.039 --> 00:03:37.360
when we're talking about replication,

55
00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:41.199
one of the things that's often forgotten is that people can enjoin,

56
00:03:41.199 --> 00:03:43.240
depending on your game setup obviously,

57
00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.400
it's often a problem you hit up against

58
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.560
that people can join your game mid-session.

59
00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:53.159
And so it's worth considering how you are passing data around

60
00:03:53.159 --> 00:03:57.560
such that you maintain some kind of...

61
00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.960
All the information that is stateful

62
00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.439
isn't purely replicated through like function calls,

63
00:04:03.439 --> 00:04:05.560
isn't just, oh, this information has changed

64
00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:08.759
and therefore I'll just let everyone who's currently connected know

65
00:04:08.759 --> 00:04:11.240
that you maintain some kind of persistent state

66
00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.680
for all of the bits of information you care about.

67
00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.399
And therefore, when a new player possibly joins 10 minutes

68
00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:20.480
into your game, they get all the bits of information

69
00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:23.720
they actually need and they don't get some kind of smaller subset

70
00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.519
where you've got a mismatch between players that were here

71
00:04:26.519 --> 00:04:29.360
for this initial bit of information getting broadcast out

72
00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.740
and then the players who join afterwards who kind of missed that.

73
00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.100
So what can be replicated?

74
00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:42.540
Essentially, Unreal provides a lot of data types

75
00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.980
that can be replicated out of the box.

76
00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:47.459
The vast majority of data types are replicated,

77
00:04:47.459 --> 00:04:49.220
but some are completely not.

78
00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:50.860
And others require a little bit of nuance

79
00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:53.699
and kind of understanding about how they actually work.

80
00:04:53.699 --> 00:04:56.259
So straight off the bat, our atomic types,

81
00:04:56.259 --> 00:04:59.459
our kind of fundamental types, bools, ints, floats,

82
00:04:59.459 --> 00:05:02.019
all of these are going to be replicated.

83
00:05:02.019 --> 00:05:05.699
Unreal also supports struct replication,

84
00:05:05.699 --> 00:05:08.180
as long as the information in those structs

85
00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:11.019
are one of these types that are already replicated.

86
00:05:11.019 --> 00:05:15.019
It's worth mentioning that when we're dealing with structs,

87
00:05:15.899 --> 00:05:17.660
as a kind of efficiency thing,

88
00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:20.779
if you are changing information in a struct,

89
00:05:20.779 --> 00:05:23.300
you don't need to change the overall struct.

90
00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.620
We'll get into this a little bit, and then I've got an example

91
00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:27.779
that shows this right at the end of the presentation.

92
00:05:27.779 --> 00:05:31.899
But you can change individual bits of information in a struct,

93
00:05:31.899 --> 00:05:34.860
and Unreal will understand that it's only that bit of information

94
00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:38.980
that has changed and therefore only send that unique bit of information

95
00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:42.939
within the struct over to the client.

96
00:05:42.939 --> 00:05:46.699
Or if you're communicating that data back to the server

97
00:05:46.699 --> 00:05:49.300
somehow the same way.

98
00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:52.699
So you don't have to make a change to the whole struct

99
00:05:52.699 --> 00:05:54.660
to say, oh, this will get replicated.

100
00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.459
If a struct is replicated and you only change one member

101
00:05:57.459 --> 00:05:59.699
within that struct, it is only that member

102
00:05:59.699 --> 00:06:03.139
that will get passed over to the other clients.

103
00:06:03.139 --> 00:06:07.019
Enums, obviously, even though they are kind of specialized types,

104
00:06:07.019 --> 00:06:10.860
they are, under the surface, they are really a UN8,

105
00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:14.540
and so they will be sent over the network

106
00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.740
just as an atomic type, really.

107
00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:19.420
Arrays are also supported,

108
00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:22.019
and they're kind of smart things done about arrays

109
00:06:22.019 --> 00:06:27.699
where if you make an overall change to an array,

110
00:06:27.699 --> 00:06:29.899
then it's your first time,

111
00:06:29.899 --> 00:06:31.660
then the whole array will be copied over.

112
00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:34.939
But if you make changes to elements within an array

113
00:06:34.939 --> 00:06:36.459
or you add a single element,

114
00:06:36.459 --> 00:06:38.379
it's only those individual changes

115
00:06:38.379 --> 00:06:41.540
that will get replicated over to the clients.

116
00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:44.100
So, again, that is kind of an efficiency

117
00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:45.300
that is already being done for you,

118
00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.740
so you don't need to be super clever with these things.

119
00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.660
It's worth mentioning, though, that when you are doing,

120
00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:51.420
we've not talked about RPCs yet,

121
00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:52.819
but when you are doing RPCs

122
00:06:52.819 --> 00:06:54.779
and you're passing RPCs with arrays

123
00:06:54.779 --> 00:06:58.500
and you're passing an array in as a parameter of that RPC,

124
00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:01.220
the whole array will be copied over,

125
00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:02.139
and you can't avoid that.

126
00:07:02.139 --> 00:07:05.100
So just kind of a kind of anti-optimization

127
00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.939
that's worth bearing in mind.

128
00:07:08.339 --> 00:07:10.579
As with a lot of Tmaps,

129
00:07:10.579 --> 00:07:12.779
they're unfortunately a little bit underrepresented

130
00:07:12.779 --> 00:07:14.899
in lots of different aspects,

131
00:07:14.899 --> 00:07:16.740
and replication is one of those.

132
00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:20.980
So if you kind of want Tmap functionality,

133
00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:23.740
one of the easy things that you can do,

134
00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:26.019
for anyone who's not familiar with the term map,

135
00:07:26.019 --> 00:07:28.620
there might be other terms that you've used for this,

136
00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:32.779
but this is essentially the idea that you have a container,

137
00:07:32.779 --> 00:07:36.500
you know, a type that contains multiple entries,

138
00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:38.459
but rather than being represented by an index,

139
00:07:38.459 --> 00:07:41.259
like an array, it's represented by a key type.

140
00:07:41.259 --> 00:07:42.620
So you give it some kind of type

141
00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.980
that represents a unique value,

142
00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:47.180
and then it maps to a value.

143
00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:50.300
So it's otherwise called key value pairs.

144
00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:53.699
And if you need that kind of map-like functionality,

145
00:07:53.699 --> 00:07:55.939
but you want it to be replicated,

146
00:07:55.939 --> 00:07:57.139
you can do it via an array,

147
00:07:57.139 --> 00:08:01.339
and if you override your,

148
00:08:01.339 --> 00:08:03.220
in C++ at least,

149
00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:04.939
if you override your functionality

150
00:08:04.939 --> 00:08:08.060
to have an equality operator check,

151
00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:11.420
then you can easily kind of find within that array.

152
00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:14.259
It's not, there are performance differences, obviously,

153
00:08:14.259 --> 00:08:16.060
but you get a lot of that same functionality.

154
00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:19.779
And you can also use index of by key or find by key,

155
00:08:19.779 --> 00:08:22.540
even if you don't want to do a direct

156
00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:25.459
struct to struct comparison or value to value comparison,

157
00:08:25.459 --> 00:08:27.540
you can use key types.

158
00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:29.860
So just be aware that there are kind of like

159
00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:32.779
ways around that for usage purposes,

160
00:08:32.779 --> 00:08:35.700
but obviously there is a kind of meaningful

161
00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:38.899
performance differences between a map and an array.

162
00:08:38.899 --> 00:08:42.659
So, you know, it's not a perfect,

163
00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:44.620
replication is not the right word to use there,

164
00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:47.100
but it's not a one-to-one relationship,

165
00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:50.139
but you can get a lot of the way there.

166
00:08:50.139 --> 00:08:52.100
To all intents and purposes,

167
00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:57.700
pointers are supported for replication in a sense,

168
00:08:57.700 --> 00:08:59.500
but the things that are,

169
00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:02.820
the pointers that are replicated are only pointers

170
00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:06.620
to objects that are themselves replicated.

171
00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:08.340
So you kind of need to have a knowledge of,

172
00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:10.659
you know, all the data in your game that is replicated

173
00:09:10.659 --> 00:09:13.259
to be know whether or not the object

174
00:09:13.259 --> 00:09:15.700
that you're talking about is going to be kind of valid

175
00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:17.779
to be replicated itself.

176
00:09:17.779 --> 00:09:21.500
Essentially, when objects are replicated,

177
00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:23.379
they are kind of given a unique ID

178
00:09:23.379 --> 00:09:26.659
and that unique ID is itself replicated from server to client.

179
00:09:26.659 --> 00:09:28.860
So it is consistent between the two.

180
00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:31.620
And therefore, when you're passing a pointer around,

181
00:09:31.659 --> 00:09:34.179
actually what gets replicated over the server

182
00:09:34.179 --> 00:09:38.460
is that unique ID, and therefore it's easy for your client

183
00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:40.179
to then rebuild that same connection and be like,

184
00:09:40.179 --> 00:09:41.700
oh, they've passed in this unique ID,

185
00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:44.820
I know what object has that unique ID

186
00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:48.139
and therefore I'll hook that up on my local side.

187
00:09:48.139 --> 00:09:49.100
So that that's how it works

188
00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:52.019
and therefore pointers do work, they can be replicated,

189
00:09:52.019 --> 00:09:54.299
but only pointers to objects

190
00:09:54.299 --> 00:09:57.179
that are themselves replicated.

191
00:09:57.179 --> 00:09:59.620
So how do we even make something replicated?

192
00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:02.860
We've talked about kind of what replication itself means,

193
00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:04.740
but how do we actually do this?

194
00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:09.340
So there is essentially just a variable inside actors.

195
00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:11.980
Like I said, actors are our kind of key entry point

196
00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:15.059
for all of our kind of replication logic.

197
00:10:15.059 --> 00:10:18.500
And there's essentially just a bool that represents

198
00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:20.059
whether or not an actor is replicated.

199
00:10:20.059 --> 00:10:21.899
If you just go to your actor,

200
00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:26.539
there is a replicates, I think it's called.

201
00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:29.960
Yeah, replicates.

202
00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:32.360
And this can be true or false by default.

203
00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:34.600
You can also set it at runtime

204
00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:36.840
through a function called set replicates.

205
00:10:37.759 --> 00:10:40.799
There are gonna be certain consequences to that.

206
00:10:40.799 --> 00:10:43.320
It's not necessarily advisable

207
00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:45.360
that you update these things at runtime

208
00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:47.799
because there's probably gonna be

209
00:10:47.799 --> 00:10:50.879
a lot of kind of weird consequences of that.

210
00:10:50.879 --> 00:10:52.480
So the vast majority of the time,

211
00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:55.480
you should be doing it within the body of the,

212
00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:58.320
if you are gonna be doing set replicates true,

213
00:10:58.320 --> 00:10:59.559
if you are gonna be calling that function,

214
00:11:00.159 --> 00:11:02.759
you should probably be doing that inside your constructor.

215
00:11:02.759 --> 00:11:06.159
But there probably are kind of workarounds

216
00:11:06.159 --> 00:11:07.840
where you could hypothetically hack into that

217
00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:11.039
and replicate that replicatedness over the server,

218
00:11:11.039 --> 00:11:12.639
but that would be a little bit weird.

219
00:11:12.639 --> 00:11:13.799
The vast majority of the time,

220
00:11:13.799 --> 00:11:15.480
an actor should, this should be true

221
00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:18.759
if an actor ever replicates and false if not.

222
00:11:18.759 --> 00:11:20.759
And that's basically our starting point

223
00:11:20.759 --> 00:11:22.720
for whether or not this thing can,

224
00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:24.519
none of the rest of this is relevant

225
00:11:24.519 --> 00:11:27.039
if the actor is not itself relevant.

226
00:11:27.039 --> 00:11:29.440
And also with a component,

227
00:11:29.519 --> 00:11:31.320
we have a similar variable.

228
00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:33.639
And similarly, we've got that function

229
00:11:33.639 --> 00:11:35.720
should be used done within the constructor.

230
00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:38.440
If a component replicates

231
00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:40.240
is not on an actor that replicates,

232
00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:41.639
then it's completely irrelevant.

233
00:11:41.639 --> 00:11:43.559
Components can only be replicated

234
00:11:43.559 --> 00:11:46.659
when they are on an actor that replicates.

235
00:11:46.659 --> 00:11:49.940
And then once something is replicated,

236
00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:51.500
then that just means that this thing

237
00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:53.980
can make use of replication functionality,

238
00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:56.580
but there are additional steps that we have to take

239
00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:59.740
whenever we actually want to mark something as replicatable.

240
00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:03.220
So first off, if it's data,

241
00:12:04.259 --> 00:12:06.019
you know, typically when we're talking about replication

242
00:12:06.019 --> 00:12:08.779
that we're talking about like actual variable replication.

243
00:12:08.779 --> 00:12:12.500
And that is, we would do,

244
00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:13.700
we would have to have types

245
00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:15.860
that can be marked up with a U property.

246
00:12:15.860 --> 00:12:18.659
So we know what this means from previous sessions

247
00:12:18.659 --> 00:12:19.620
that, you know, you really,

248
00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:22.019
you can only mark something up as a U property

249
00:12:22.019 --> 00:12:24.500
if it's inside either a U struct or a U class

250
00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:26.340
as we've spoken about.

251
00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:28.059
So all of this is only applies

252
00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:29.379
to anything that inherits

253
00:12:29.379 --> 00:12:34.539
is part of the U object hierarchy chain.

254
00:12:34.539 --> 00:12:36.500
And you would just add,

255
00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:38.179
if you just want data that is replicated

256
00:12:38.179 --> 00:12:39.820
from server to client,

257
00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:43.940
you would just put the replicated markup above it.

258
00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:46.539
And the other option is replicated using,

259
00:12:46.539 --> 00:12:48.700
which basically gives us replication,

260
00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:50.299
but with additional callbacks.

261
00:12:51.179 --> 00:12:53.100
So with replicated using,

262
00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:55.019
what you do is you, this is essentially saying

263
00:12:55.019 --> 00:12:57.779
this data does get replicated from server to client,

264
00:12:57.779 --> 00:12:59.419
but additionally to that,

265
00:13:01.059 --> 00:13:03.539
there is a function that will be called

266
00:13:03.539 --> 00:13:05.259
and here is what that function is called.

267
00:13:05.259 --> 00:13:06.580
The name does matter.

268
00:13:06.580 --> 00:13:07.779
I'll go into this in a little bit,

269
00:13:07.779 --> 00:13:08.620
but it does need,

270
00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:12.220
this onRep underscore is a kind of requirement,

271
00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:14.220
but then what you call the function after that

272
00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:15.340
is kind of completely up to you.

273
00:13:15.340 --> 00:13:17.460
It doesn't have to have any correlation

274
00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:20.379
with the actual data that you are replicating.

275
00:13:20.379 --> 00:13:25.379
It's worth noting that data won't be replicated

276
00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:27.820
if it's the same, if it's already,

277
00:13:28.419 --> 00:13:29.259
if you make a change on the server

278
00:13:29.259 --> 00:13:31.980
and it's not changed from what the client thinks,

279
00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:35.179
then that information is not going to get replicated over.

280
00:13:36.059 --> 00:13:38.100
So it's worth bearing that in mind

281
00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:39.779
in the context of onRep,

282
00:13:39.779 --> 00:13:43.460
if you're relying on your onRep function to get called,

283
00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:46.019
but you haven't actually changed the data,

284
00:13:46.019 --> 00:13:50.059
that's not going to change anything.

285
00:13:50.059 --> 00:13:53.340
It's also worth mentioning that if you are relying

286
00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:55.700
on your onRep functions getting called,

287
00:13:55.740 --> 00:13:58.299
if you are in single-player,

288
00:13:59.419 --> 00:14:03.340
and I believe also on your Listen server,

289
00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:05.779
then your onRep functions won't necessarily get called.

290
00:14:05.779 --> 00:14:07.980
So you kind of need to account for that.

291
00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:10.460
On replication is only really for when data

292
00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:13.539
is getting passed from the server to a client.

293
00:14:13.539 --> 00:14:14.659
So when you're in standalone,

294
00:14:14.659 --> 00:14:16.139
that relationship kind of isn't there

295
00:14:16.139 --> 00:14:18.019
and therefore your onRep functions

296
00:14:18.019 --> 00:14:19.019
won't be getting called.

297
00:14:19.019 --> 00:14:20.620
So you need to account for that

298
00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:23.379
and possibly develop alternative avenues

299
00:14:23.379 --> 00:14:24.980
if your game is a game that can be played

300
00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:26.460
both single-player and multiplayer,

301
00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:30.639
and you're relying on this onRep functionality.

302
00:14:30.639 --> 00:14:32.440
The other thing that you will need to do

303
00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:35.320
is put any variables.

304
00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:37.320
So replicated and replicated using

305
00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:38.960
are not enough on their own.

306
00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:43.080
You also have this function,

307
00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:46.200
which is get lifetime replicated properties,

308
00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:49.519
and you basically need to pass in some macros

309
00:14:49.519 --> 00:14:50.879
to essentially say,

310
00:14:50.879 --> 00:14:53.960
this is data that will get replicated.

311
00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:56.480
And you're essentially going to be passing in

312
00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:59.240
the same variables that you've put replication markup above.

313
00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:01.759
You're going to pass in the name of the class

314
00:15:01.759 --> 00:15:03.039
that this is within,

315
00:15:03.039 --> 00:15:05.200
and then the name of the variable itself.

316
00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:06.879
I've not actually spent a huge amount of time

317
00:15:06.879 --> 00:15:07.960
looking into the macro itself,

318
00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:10.000
but I suspect what this is doing,

319
00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:11.759
there are other parts of the engine that do this,

320
00:15:11.759 --> 00:15:14.480
is I suspect that this is doing a compile time checking

321
00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:17.039
just to make sure that you've spelled this name correctly.

322
00:15:17.039 --> 00:15:20.320
And therefore, if you're, you know,

323
00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:21.840
this is called sumVar,

324
00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:23.799
and then you just make a editor change

325
00:15:23.799 --> 00:15:26.679
and you change that to sumVar2,

326
00:15:26.679 --> 00:15:29.240
I suspect it's going to be a compile time check

327
00:15:29.240 --> 00:15:31.080
so that the compiler will complain at you to say,

328
00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:34.559
hey, you've not correctly renamed this.

329
00:15:34.559 --> 00:15:37.799
There's no variable called sumVar anymore.

330
00:15:37.799 --> 00:15:40.940
So, you know, you need to update that.

331
00:15:40.940 --> 00:15:43.500
There are alternative options as well.

332
00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:46.539
It's not just the do rep lifetime macro.

333
00:15:46.539 --> 00:15:49.100
There is an additional one called do rep,

334
00:15:49.100 --> 00:15:50.539
there are a couple of actually,

335
00:15:50.539 --> 00:15:52.460
but the other kind of one that's worth bearing in mind

336
00:15:52.460 --> 00:15:55.419
is the do rep lifetime condition,

337
00:15:55.419 --> 00:15:58.659
which lets us specify some kind of additional parameters

338
00:15:58.659 --> 00:16:01.620
where this should only replicate across the client

339
00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:03.419
in certain situations.

340
00:16:03.419 --> 00:16:04.620
We've actually got an example,

341
00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:06.980
so we'll come to kind of,

342
00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:08.659
the reason I've actually made this

343
00:16:08.659 --> 00:16:11.659
is for demoing a couple of other,

344
00:16:11.659 --> 00:16:14.100
I'm sorry, jumping to the wrong actor there,

345
00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:16.340
demoing this for a couple of different reasons,

346
00:16:16.340 --> 00:16:17.580
and we'll kind of get towards this

347
00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:19.539
at the very end of the session,

348
00:16:19.539 --> 00:16:22.620
but just to kind of show some of this in situ,

349
00:16:22.620 --> 00:16:25.019
as you can see, we've got a float here.

350
00:16:25.019 --> 00:16:26.580
This is inside a U class.

351
00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:28.700
Obviously, again, we can't have U properties

352
00:16:28.700 --> 00:16:32.220
if it's not inside either a U struct or a U class.

353
00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:35.019
So we have an actor here.

354
00:16:35.019 --> 00:16:36.620
I'll actually demo this actor,

355
00:16:36.620 --> 00:16:38.659
like I said, at the end of the session,

356
00:16:38.659 --> 00:16:40.659
but we've got a variable here.

357
00:16:40.659 --> 00:16:42.259
I wanted to replicate this variable.

358
00:16:42.259 --> 00:16:44.740
Therefore, I have to do a U property markup above it.

359
00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:46.860
I have to say it's replicated,

360
00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:48.899
and then that alone is not enough.

361
00:16:48.899 --> 00:16:52.259
I have to make sure that that is inside an actor

362
00:16:52.259 --> 00:16:55.059
that is replicated or a component that is replicated

363
00:16:55.059 --> 00:16:56.700
on an actor that is replicated.

364
00:16:57.820 --> 00:16:59.500
And so you can see,

365
00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:03.889
if I jump to my constructor for my actor,

366
00:17:03.889 --> 00:17:05.609
you can see I've done all the relevant things.

367
00:17:05.609 --> 00:17:07.890
We'll talk about relevancy in a moment,

368
00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:09.609
but you can see that in the constructor,

369
00:17:09.609 --> 00:17:11.809
we've said this actor replicates.

370
00:17:11.809 --> 00:17:14.730
And the other thing we've done is added,

371
00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:17.490
saying I do rep lifetime.

372
00:17:17.490 --> 00:17:19.250
We've passed in our actor class,

373
00:17:19.250 --> 00:17:21.130
and we've passed in the variable name,

374
00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:22.809
so sum replicated float.

375
00:17:22.809 --> 00:17:25.490
All of that are the things that you need to do

376
00:17:25.490 --> 00:17:26.809
to make sure that this variable

377
00:17:26.809 --> 00:17:29.049
will actually get replicated.

378
00:17:29.049 --> 00:17:30.329
And then here we have,

379
00:17:30.329 --> 00:17:33.930
we're making use of this on rep, this rep using.

380
00:17:33.930 --> 00:17:36.569
So you don't need to say replicated and rep using.

381
00:17:36.569 --> 00:17:37.609
Rep using can be done.

382
00:17:37.609 --> 00:17:40.210
It inherently implies that this value is replicated.

383
00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:41.609
And then you pass in the function

384
00:17:41.609 --> 00:17:43.569
that will actually get called

385
00:17:43.569 --> 00:17:45.490
when this value gets replicated to.

386
00:17:46.410 --> 00:17:51.410
So this, again, the name here doesn't have any bearing

387
00:17:51.849 --> 00:17:53.329
on the actual data that's replicated.

388
00:17:53.329 --> 00:17:55.009
You're just saying this function will get called

389
00:17:55.009 --> 00:17:57.210
when this value gets replicated into.

390
00:17:58.849 --> 00:18:00.849
But the on rep is important.

391
00:18:00.849 --> 00:18:03.490
So this function is just down here.

392
00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:07.250
It is important that this function is a U function.

393
00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:10.329
If it isn't a U function, it won't get properly found.

394
00:18:10.329 --> 00:18:13.250
This is all using the Unreal Reflection system.

395
00:18:13.250 --> 00:18:16.049
So this function has to be a U function.

396
00:18:18.109 --> 00:18:20.069
And similarly, you can see that,

397
00:18:20.069 --> 00:18:21.430
when inside that function,

398
00:18:21.430 --> 00:18:23.430
we don't need to go into great detail about this,

399
00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:24.990
but we're just basically printing a string.

400
00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:26.950
So when this value gets updated

401
00:18:26.950 --> 00:18:28.829
and replicated onto the client,

402
00:18:28.829 --> 00:18:31.069
that value, we're gonna do some printing.

403
00:18:31.069 --> 00:18:35.910
And similarly, we have to do for replicated using values

404
00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:36.950
as well as replicated values,

405
00:18:36.950 --> 00:18:38.470
we have to add them to this

406
00:18:38.470 --> 00:18:40.349
get lifetime replicated props.

407
00:18:41.269 --> 00:18:43.910
You also wanna be sure that you call your super function

408
00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:45.990
here because there's gonna be data further down the chain

409
00:18:45.990 --> 00:18:46.950
that is also replicated.

410
00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:48.869
So you don't want to forget to call that

411
00:18:48.869 --> 00:18:52.230
so that the parent class functions are getting called.

412
00:18:52.230 --> 00:18:54.309
And then here, we've got this example

413
00:18:54.309 --> 00:18:56.829
of the do rep lifetime condition.

414
00:18:56.829 --> 00:19:00.230
This condition, there's a whole suite of these

415
00:19:00.230 --> 00:19:02.430
that you can kind of look at,

416
00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:05.789
but so you can kind of jump into cornet types.h

417
00:19:05.789 --> 00:19:07.150
and look at those.

418
00:19:07.150 --> 00:19:08.910
But the example that I've given

419
00:19:08.950 --> 00:19:11.549
is this particular type,

420
00:19:11.549 --> 00:19:14.230
the sum replicated struct variable,

421
00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:16.869
we've marked it to only get replicated

422
00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:19.509
for simulated actors.

423
00:19:19.509 --> 00:19:21.549
We'll talk about what that kind of meaning is,

424
00:19:21.549 --> 00:19:24.670
but it essentially means that on the owning,

425
00:19:24.670 --> 00:19:26.990
on the person, on the connection on the client

426
00:19:26.990 --> 00:19:29.190
that actually owns this actor,

427
00:19:29.190 --> 00:19:31.309
i.e. for the case of a pawn,

428
00:19:31.309 --> 00:19:34.549
that the client that is actually controlling this pawn,

429
00:19:34.549 --> 00:19:36.470
this data won't get replicated to them.

430
00:19:36.470 --> 00:19:38.029
So this particular condition is one

431
00:19:38.029 --> 00:19:39.910
that you might use if it's data

432
00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:42.990
that you know will actually kind of come from the client.

433
00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:45.109
So say, for example,

434
00:19:45.109 --> 00:19:47.390
your client is making local changes

435
00:19:47.390 --> 00:19:48.470
to their customization.

436
00:19:48.470 --> 00:19:50.109
So they've changed what hat they're wearing

437
00:19:50.109 --> 00:19:52.750
and they make that change locally on their client

438
00:19:52.750 --> 00:19:54.990
and then replicate that data to the server.

439
00:19:56.390 --> 00:19:57.829
Therefore, that might be data

440
00:19:57.829 --> 00:19:59.670
that the server doesn't need to replicate back

441
00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:02.349
to the client because they know the client

442
00:20:02.349 --> 00:20:04.309
through other alternative means

443
00:20:04.309 --> 00:20:07.190
will have the most up-to-date idea of that information.

444
00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:09.150
And therefore, they only need to replicate it

445
00:20:09.150 --> 00:20:12.710
to the clients that aren't owning this actor itself.

446
00:20:12.710 --> 00:20:15.029
That's just an example of the kind of condition

447
00:20:15.029 --> 00:20:17.029
you might want to place on your variables

448
00:20:17.029 --> 00:20:18.150
when they get replicated.

449
00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:19.869
Otherwise, this is basically saying,

450
00:20:19.869 --> 00:20:22.269
replicate this data to all clients

451
00:20:22.269 --> 00:20:24.190
whenever they pass other checks

452
00:20:24.190 --> 00:20:30.150
that we'll get to in a little bit.

453
00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:34.390
The other thing to bear in mind with any passing data

454
00:20:34.390 --> 00:20:37.509
from the server to the client is it takes time.

455
00:20:37.509 --> 00:20:39.549
Obviously, these things aren't instantaneous.

456
00:20:39.549 --> 00:20:42.630
When testing, sometimes it can feel that way,

457
00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:46.069
but depending on how far away your server is

458
00:20:46.069 --> 00:20:48.390
from your client and depending on their internet speed,

459
00:20:48.390 --> 00:20:51.549
you could be talking fractional seconds,

460
00:20:51.549 --> 00:20:53.230
you could be talking multiple seconds.

461
00:20:53.230 --> 00:20:54.789
So do bear that in mind.

462
00:20:54.789 --> 00:20:57.069
Don't rely on things being instantaneous

463
00:20:57.069 --> 00:20:58.150
because they are not.

464
00:20:59.529 --> 00:21:01.210
So when we're talking about function calls,

465
00:21:01.210 --> 00:21:03.250
we've talked about the idea of passing data

466
00:21:03.250 --> 00:21:06.289
over replicated data, but we also have RPCs.

467
00:21:07.289 --> 00:21:11.450
RPCs are a term that is not necessarily unique to Unreal.

468
00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:15.450
This is something that exists in other engines.

469
00:21:15.450 --> 00:21:17.569
I think it's a kind of general term,

470
00:21:17.569 --> 00:21:18.970
but if you're not familiar with it,

471
00:21:18.970 --> 00:21:20.890
RPC stands for remote procedural.

472
00:21:20.890 --> 00:21:24.289
It's essentially a little bit like a function

473
00:21:24.289 --> 00:21:25.930
that you send over the network.

474
00:21:28.359 --> 00:21:31.480
So we can, there's a couple of different,

475
00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:32.960
RPCs aren't one thing.

476
00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:35.799
It's kind of a whole subset of things.

477
00:21:35.799 --> 00:21:38.440
We can send, we can do a function call

478
00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:41.680
from, we can go from a client to a server.

479
00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:45.359
We can go from the server to a specific client,

480
00:21:45.359 --> 00:21:48.200
as long as we are calling that function on an object

481
00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:50.640
that's ultimately owned by a particular client.

482
00:21:52.589 --> 00:21:55.349
Each player controller, as we kind of all the way back here,

483
00:21:55.349 --> 00:21:56.910
we kind of said, we've got this relationship

484
00:21:56.910 --> 00:21:58.990
where the server has every player controller

485
00:21:58.990 --> 00:22:01.829
and then every client has their own player controller

486
00:22:01.829 --> 00:22:02.950
and no one else's.

487
00:22:02.950 --> 00:22:04.109
So that's, as I said,

488
00:22:04.109 --> 00:22:06.509
this kind of one-to-one unique connection

489
00:22:06.509 --> 00:22:08.470
that each client has with the server.

490
00:22:09.349 --> 00:22:12.309
So that's where this kind of comes in here,

491
00:22:12.309 --> 00:22:15.029
is that when we're doing kind of client function calls,

492
00:22:15.029 --> 00:22:18.750
that is, those are to be done on objects

493
00:22:18.750 --> 00:22:19.950
that they are only gonna get called

494
00:22:19.950 --> 00:22:23.150
on that unique player controller.

495
00:22:23.150 --> 00:22:26.859
So each player controller kind of represents

496
00:22:26.859 --> 00:22:29.579
that unique connection between the client

497
00:22:29.579 --> 00:22:31.700
and the controlling server.

498
00:22:31.700 --> 00:22:34.019
So when a client is communicating information

499
00:22:34.019 --> 00:22:35.460
back to the server,

500
00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:37.500
they can only do it through their player controller

501
00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:38.980
and obviously they can only do it

502
00:22:38.980 --> 00:22:42.579
about objects that they are the owner for.

503
00:22:42.579 --> 00:22:45.019
And then we have this kind of third type.

504
00:22:45.019 --> 00:22:47.700
So we have, you know, client to server,

505
00:22:47.700 --> 00:22:49.099
obviously that can only be done

506
00:22:49.099 --> 00:22:51.420
on objects that the client owns.

507
00:22:51.420 --> 00:22:53.339
Server to client, that's with the intention

508
00:22:53.339 --> 00:22:55.220
that we're kind of communicating that information

509
00:22:55.220 --> 00:22:56.700
to an individual client,

510
00:22:56.700 --> 00:22:58.579
also through their player controller.

511
00:22:58.579 --> 00:23:00.700
And then we have this kind of net multicast.

512
00:23:00.700 --> 00:23:01.539
This is a little bit,

513
00:23:01.539 --> 00:23:06.470
we talked about delegates, I believe in our second session,

514
00:23:06.470 --> 00:23:08.269
we have the idea of multicast delegates.

515
00:23:08.269 --> 00:23:10.430
So that's the kind of information

516
00:23:10.430 --> 00:23:12.309
that is just broadcast everywhere.

517
00:23:12.309 --> 00:23:14.309
It's the same kind of concept here.

518
00:23:14.309 --> 00:23:16.269
And this is basically a function

519
00:23:16.269 --> 00:23:18.869
where it's not from client to server,

520
00:23:18.869 --> 00:23:20.789
it's only of a server to client.

521
00:23:20.789 --> 00:23:24.069
And this is a kind of shotgun function

522
00:23:24.069 --> 00:23:26.349
that broadcasts to every single client

523
00:23:26.349 --> 00:23:28.990
and therefore require no player controller

524
00:23:28.990 --> 00:23:33.289
because they're just gonna let every client know about this.

525
00:23:33.289 --> 00:23:34.730
So the way that we would do this

526
00:23:34.730 --> 00:23:37.650
is similar to how we had to do U property markup

527
00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:39.809
with replication.

528
00:23:39.809 --> 00:23:43.450
For RPCs, we have U function markup.

529
00:23:43.450 --> 00:23:46.049
So this implies certain things, right?

530
00:23:46.049 --> 00:23:48.569
For a function to be an RPC,

531
00:23:48.569 --> 00:23:50.210
it has to be a U function.

532
00:23:50.210 --> 00:23:51.569
And for U functions to exist,

533
00:23:51.569 --> 00:23:55.730
they have to exist within a U struct or a U class.

534
00:23:55.730 --> 00:23:59.009
So all of that logic all kind of bubbles down.

535
00:24:00.819 --> 00:24:04.180
And yeah, we can specify one of those three configurations.

536
00:24:04.180 --> 00:24:09.940
We can specify if it's a client, a server, or a net multicast.

537
00:24:09.940 --> 00:24:11.819
The other thing that we can mark up

538
00:24:11.819 --> 00:24:16.099
is whether or not a function is reliable or unreliable.

539
00:24:16.099 --> 00:24:21.380
So yeah, in the context of this,

540
00:24:21.380 --> 00:24:28.380
again, is not necessarily unique to Unreal,

541
00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:30.539
but reliable functions are essentially functions

542
00:24:30.579 --> 00:24:35.019
that you can trust will always make their way to the client.

543
00:24:35.019 --> 00:24:36.900
They may not necessarily get there instantaneously.

544
00:24:36.900 --> 00:24:38.180
They may take time.

545
00:24:38.180 --> 00:24:40.619
If the client has some kind of package loss

546
00:24:40.619 --> 00:24:42.700
or if their connection gets disrupted,

547
00:24:42.700 --> 00:24:46.140
it might take quite a bit of time.

548
00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:48.339
And unreliable functions are essentially functions

549
00:24:48.339 --> 00:24:50.339
that, you know, due to packet loss,

550
00:24:50.339 --> 00:24:55.500
due to connection problems, there may be a kind of like,

551
00:24:55.500 --> 00:24:58.339
essentially this function might get lost.

552
00:24:58.339 --> 00:25:00.059
So you need to kind of bear that in mind

553
00:25:00.059 --> 00:25:03.420
when you're marking functions as reliable or unreliable.

554
00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:06.700
Reliable functions aren't just magically, you know,

555
00:25:06.700 --> 00:25:10.180
the temptation can just be to mark every function as reliable.

556
00:25:10.180 --> 00:25:12.500
But actually, the general advice

557
00:25:12.500 --> 00:25:15.259
is to make as many things unreliable as possible,

558
00:25:15.259 --> 00:25:18.660
only the absolute most key functions to make reliable.

559
00:25:18.660 --> 00:25:21.779
And that's because reliable kind of has extra cost.

560
00:25:21.779 --> 00:25:24.339
When functions are marked as reliable,

561
00:25:24.339 --> 00:25:25.619
they kind of enter a queue

562
00:25:25.619 --> 00:25:27.660
and they kind of maintain both on the server

563
00:25:27.660 --> 00:25:29.700
and, you know, it's almost like it maintains

564
00:25:29.700 --> 00:25:31.500
that shadow set of data to make sure

565
00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:34.420
that this data does actually make its way to the client.

566
00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:40.259
So they are more expensive to both call and maintain.

567
00:25:40.259 --> 00:25:43.339
So therefore, if a function can be unreliable,

568
00:25:43.339 --> 00:25:44.859
it should be unreliable.

569
00:25:44.859 --> 00:25:47.140
But you just have to kind of understand that,

570
00:25:47.140 --> 00:25:49.619
you know, there is a world where that function

571
00:25:49.619 --> 00:25:53.700
never makes its way to the kind of receiving client

572
00:25:53.700 --> 00:25:57.730
if there are connection problems or anything like that.

573
00:25:57.730 --> 00:26:02.130
The other thing that to kind of bear in mind

574
00:26:02.130 --> 00:26:06.210
is that we've also got this capability for validation.

575
00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:08.849
So validation is additional markup

576
00:26:08.849 --> 00:26:14.009
that we can do on the RPC.

577
00:26:14.009 --> 00:26:17.130
And essentially, it lets us specify

578
00:26:17.130 --> 00:26:18.690
kind of an additional set of data.

579
00:26:18.690 --> 00:26:25.869
So with validation, as when we specified on rep,

580
00:26:25.910 --> 00:26:30.589
what that means is we can kind of add some additional function

581
00:26:30.589 --> 00:26:32.869
that will return either true or false.

582
00:26:32.869 --> 00:26:36.509
And this is basically a way of primarily

583
00:26:36.509 --> 00:26:38.859
for things like cheat detection.

584
00:26:38.859 --> 00:26:43.619
And if we return true, then it was validated.

585
00:26:43.619 --> 00:26:47.099
If we return false, then essentially the server

586
00:26:47.099 --> 00:26:49.299
will instantly kick this connection.

587
00:26:49.299 --> 00:26:53.099
So typically, you know, you want to be returning true.

588
00:26:53.099 --> 00:26:56.140
You don't want to be kicking everyone off your connection.

589
00:26:56.140 --> 00:27:00.420
But it's there for if we imagine we have an RPC

590
00:27:00.420 --> 00:27:04.539
that the client can tell the server

591
00:27:04.539 --> 00:27:06.299
they've reloaded their clip

592
00:27:06.299 --> 00:27:08.420
and this is the amount of ammo they reloaded.

593
00:27:08.420 --> 00:27:11.059
You could do some kind of simple check

594
00:27:11.059 --> 00:27:15.960
for whether or not the amount

595
00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:17.279
that they're trying to reload by

596
00:27:17.279 --> 00:27:20.599
is even a sane amount that could ever possibly be reloaded.

597
00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:24.200
And if not, maybe you can only ever reload one bullet at a time

598
00:27:24.200 --> 00:27:28.920
or you know what the maximum clip size is for their gun.

599
00:27:28.920 --> 00:27:31.759
If they're trying to reload some kind of bogus amount,

600
00:27:31.759 --> 00:27:34.400
then that's an example of how you could,

601
00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:36.839
you know, use some kind of gameplay data to validate against

602
00:27:36.839 --> 00:27:39.119
and therefore, you can assume that they're cheating,

603
00:27:39.119 --> 00:27:42.160
if not, and then they'll get kicked off.

604
00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:45.559
So that validation, unlike our RPC,

605
00:27:45.559 --> 00:27:48.799
when we do with validation,

606
00:27:48.799 --> 00:27:51.839
that we don't specify a unique function name,

607
00:27:51.839 --> 00:27:56.880
we simply have our existing function name

608
00:27:56.880 --> 00:28:01.960
and we will basically have additional markup.

609
00:28:01.960 --> 00:28:04.759
So for anyone who's used to doing delegates,

610
00:28:04.759 --> 00:28:06.079
it would look the same way.

611
00:28:06.079 --> 00:28:08.039
You have the function that you actually say,

612
00:28:08.039 --> 00:28:13.039
hey, this function is, you know, is an RPC

613
00:28:13.039 --> 00:28:14.319
and it would look like this.

614
00:28:14.319 --> 00:28:16.480
Here's its kind of signature, here's its name,

615
00:28:16.480 --> 00:28:18.079
here's the parameters it takes in.

616
00:28:18.079 --> 00:28:21.279
Then when you actually do the implementation for that

617
00:28:21.279 --> 00:28:24.079
in your C++, it would look like this.

618
00:28:24.079 --> 00:28:27.200
So you don't necessarily have to declare this in the header,

619
00:28:27.200 --> 00:28:29.960
but in your body, it would look like this.

620
00:28:29.960 --> 00:28:32.640
Your, the kind of actual header element of this

621
00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:35.720
should kind of get automatically generated for you.

622
00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:38.680
If for some reason you wanted that to be a virtual function,

623
00:28:38.680 --> 00:28:40.519
you would probably need to declare that in your header

624
00:28:40.519 --> 00:28:42.759
as a virtual version of the same function.

625
00:28:42.759 --> 00:28:44.599
But otherwise, the kind of,

626
00:28:44.599 --> 00:28:46.039
in the header that will already exist,

627
00:28:46.039 --> 00:28:46.880
but it's only in the body,

628
00:28:46.920 --> 00:28:48.279
you would need to do your function name,

629
00:28:48.279 --> 00:28:49.799
underscore implementation.

630
00:28:49.799 --> 00:28:51.960
And this is the thing that would actually get called

631
00:28:51.960 --> 00:28:54.559
when the RPC kind of filters through to the client.

632
00:28:56.250 --> 00:28:58.210
And then you would just do whatever you wanted

633
00:28:58.210 --> 00:28:59.809
your game logic to do in here.

634
00:28:59.809 --> 00:29:01.769
And then last of all, we have the validate

635
00:29:01.769 --> 00:29:02.609
and the same thing,

636
00:29:02.609 --> 00:29:07.130
it's whatever your function is called, underscore validate.

637
00:29:07.130 --> 00:29:08.450
And then this would be,

638
00:29:08.450 --> 00:29:10.490
have to be a bool function

639
00:29:10.490 --> 00:29:11.970
that returns either true or false.

640
00:29:11.970 --> 00:29:13.410
And again, if this returns true,

641
00:29:13.410 --> 00:29:14.730
then it's pass validation.

642
00:29:14.730 --> 00:29:15.849
If it returns false,

643
00:29:15.849 --> 00:29:17.289
then that connection is gonna get kicked.

644
00:29:17.289 --> 00:29:19.690
So be very careful when you're returning false.

645
00:29:22.049 --> 00:29:23.450
Yeah, I think that's everything to kind of say

646
00:29:23.450 --> 00:29:25.289
about our kind of core markup.

647
00:29:25.289 --> 00:29:27.089
So as we said already,

648
00:29:27.089 --> 00:29:29.769
we have kind of three ways of marking a function up.

649
00:29:29.769 --> 00:29:33.130
We can mark it up as server, client or multicast.

650
00:29:33.130 --> 00:29:34.490
When it's a server,

651
00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:37.609
that means it's called from the client to the server.

652
00:29:37.609 --> 00:29:41.289
So this is the client communicating data to the server.

653
00:29:41.289 --> 00:29:44.650
This should only be called on objects.

654
00:29:44.650 --> 00:29:46.170
This is essentially all through the,

655
00:29:46.170 --> 00:29:49.490
these first two are all through the player controller.

656
00:29:49.490 --> 00:29:52.529
So this should only be called on objects

657
00:29:52.529 --> 00:29:55.890
that are owned by a particular player controller.

658
00:29:55.890 --> 00:29:58.329
So, you know, an example would be your,

659
00:29:58.329 --> 00:30:00.410
the pawn that player is currently controlling.

660
00:30:00.410 --> 00:30:02.009
They are owned by that player controller

661
00:30:02.009 --> 00:30:04.529
and therefore they are owned by that connection.

662
00:30:05.809 --> 00:30:08.250
So that's the client communication information to the server

663
00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:11.730
that might, an example might be passing input along.

664
00:30:11.730 --> 00:30:14.130
I just gave an example that a player might

665
00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:17.089
on their local machine, do their customizations

666
00:30:17.089 --> 00:30:20.250
as in when they've kind of locked those customizations in

667
00:30:20.250 --> 00:30:21.970
that that might then be a server call

668
00:30:21.970 --> 00:30:23.690
to kind of communicate that information to the server

669
00:30:23.690 --> 00:30:26.250
to say, hey, server, I've updated all my customizations

670
00:30:26.250 --> 00:30:28.450
and now let the other clients know

671
00:30:28.450 --> 00:30:31.049
that my hat should look different or something like that.

672
00:30:32.289 --> 00:30:33.970
Then we have client functions.

673
00:30:33.970 --> 00:30:37.410
This is functions called on the,

674
00:30:37.410 --> 00:30:40.369
from the server to individual clients.

675
00:30:40.369 --> 00:30:42.730
Again, this is through a player controller.

676
00:30:42.730 --> 00:30:45.490
So this is about a particular owned connection.

677
00:30:45.490 --> 00:30:47.930
And then we have multicast that we already talked about.

678
00:30:47.930 --> 00:30:49.210
This is a kind of shotgun.

679
00:30:49.210 --> 00:30:51.170
This is our function that is just called everywhere.

680
00:30:51.170 --> 00:30:54.130
So this might be any data that is kind of like everyone.

681
00:30:54.130 --> 00:30:56.210
I know for a fact that every connected client

682
00:30:56.210 --> 00:30:57.609
will need to know about this.

683
00:30:57.609 --> 00:30:59.529
That might be a case for a multicast.
